On Mon, Jun 10, 2013 at 1:18 AM, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com> wrote:

> Dot Side, so far as I know, is a direct result of
> http://www.lojban.org/tiki/The+Case+Against+LA
>
> And all it does is make it so that all cmevla are always required to have
> a '.' before and after to delimit them, whereas before, cmevla which began
> with a consonant were not required to have a '.' /preceding/ them when they
> were immediately preceded by doi, la, lai, or la'i.
>
> So, not Dot-Side:
>
> mi'e.aionys.   doi.aionys.   la.aionys.
> mi'e.djan.      doi djan.       la djan.
>
> la doitclynd. -- {la doitcland.} is illegal.
>

Actually "doitclynd" was "doi tclynd". In some versions the "la" in
"tcland" was acceptable because it follows a consonant and so the word
cannot break up at that point.

mu'o mi'e xorxes

